# var-expr-init

A loop whose `var` is initialised from an EXPRESSION rather than a literal — what real
code writes whenever a loop variable comes from a parameter, a call or a computation.

**No other corpus row has this shape.** Every existing loop writes `var i = 0` and
`var sum = 0L`, i.e. literals, so the corpus measured only the case that already works.
That is why the defect below went unseen through every sweep.

Measured 2026-07-31 on the v2 bytecode lane, 1M iterations, identical arithmetic:

| initialiser | v2 ns/iter | ssc ns/iter |
|---|---|---|
| `var s = 1L` | 5.67 | 4.28 |
| `var s = (seed % …) + 1L` (this row) | **104.7** | 3.86 |

The unboxed `lcell` tier fires only on a syntactic literal, so this var gets a boxed cell
and pays on every read and write. See `TASK/v2-perfomance.md`
§v2-perf-unboxed-cell-only-for-literal-init.

Anti-fold: `seed` is opaque at the call site and drives the LCG, so no backend can
precompute the loop.

```scalascript
def workload(seed: Long): Long =
  var s = (seed % 2147483646L) + 1L
  var sum = 0L
  var i = 0
  while i < 1000000 do
    s = (s * 48271L) % 2147483647L
    sum = sum + (s % 7L)
    i = i + 1
  sum
```
